# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# build/ is TRACKED, with exceptions.
#
# The compiled artifacts are committed so a release can be audited without
# rebuilding: the jobStarr executable, libjobstarr_core.a, the 13 test binaries,
# and the deployed jobspy_bridge.py + jobspy/ package. They were produced by a
# neutral-path build, so no username or private directory layout is embedded.
#
# CMake's generated scaffolding is NOT tracked. CMakeCache.txt records the
# absolute source and binary directories of the machine that generated it, so
# committing it makes `cmake -S . -B build` hard-error for anyone who clones:
#   "The current CMakeCache.txt directory ... is different than the directory
#    ... where CMakeCache.txt was created."
# The generated makefiles and CTestTestfile.cmake embed the same absolute paths,
# so `ctest --test-dir build` cannot run from a fresh checkout either.
#
# To rebuild (always do this after cloning):
#   cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && cmake --build build -j$(nproc)
#   ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
build/CMakeCache.txt
build/Makefile
build/cmake_install.cmake
build/CTestTestfile.cmake
build/CMakeFiles/
build/DartConfiguration.tcl
build/*_autogen/
build/.qt/
build/Testing/

# Runtime configuration — NEVER COMMIT. ConfigManager::save() writes this next to
# the executable (mode 0600) with plaintext LLM API keys. The pattern is
# unanchored so it covers both ./jobStarr.yaml and build/jobStarr.yaml.
jobStarr.yaml
*.yaml.tmp

# Published application packages (generated resumes / cover letters, ODT + PDF).
output/*
!output/.gitkeep

# Python bytecode. The vendored JobSpy package in third_party/ is upstream source
# and must stay byte-identical to upstream — never commit generated .pyc files.
# Also keeps build/jobspy/ (the deployed copy) free of interpreter-specific noise.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv/
venv/

# Qt / CMake / editor noise
*.user
*.autosave
.cache/
compile_commands.json
CMakeUserPresets.json